You are given a task to integrate a component from the **kinetik** motion library into this codebase.

# Curved Wipe Transition (`gsap-curve-wipe`)

Page transition with a liquid curved wipe: an SVG path bulges up to cover the screen, the content swaps, then it peels away with the opposite bend.

## What it should do
Cover: tween path d from 'M0 100 V100 Q50 100 100 100 V100z' → 'M0 100 V50 Q50 0 100 50 V100z' (power3.in) → 'M0 100 V0 Q50 0 100 0 V100z'; swap content; reveal: 'M0 0 V50 Q50 100 100 50 V0z' → 'M0 0 V0 Q50 0 100 0 V0z' (power2.out) while the new screen rises 60px→0.

## Project requirements
- React 19 + TypeScript, Tailwind CSS v4, shadcn/ui-style CSS variables (`background`, `foreground`, `muted`, `muted-foreground`, `border`, `card`, `primary`, `ring`). If the project lacks shadcn, set it up first (`npx shadcn@latest init`).
- Animation engine: **gsap**.
- Install dependencies: `npm i gsap @gsap/react`
- Put the component at `components/kinetik/gsap-curve-wipe.tsx`.
- `cn` lives at `lib/kinetik/utils.ts` (source below).
- Shared hooks go at `lib/kinetik/core.ts` (source below).

## Non-negotiables
- **Accessibility:** Overlay is aria-hidden; nav buttons expose aria-current. Reduced motion swaps content instantly.
- **Performance:** attr:{d} tweens on one path; no plugin required.
- Respect `prefers-reduced-motion` (replace movement with opacity or an instant change).
- Animate only transform / opacity / filter / clip-path. No hard-coded theme colors — use CSS variables so it works in light and dark.

